      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 The Society of Thoracic Surgeons. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "A 42-year-old man experienced a complex aortic arch aneurysm rupture. He had previously undergone an operation for type A dissection and had extremely poor cardiac performance as well as deep sternal infection after the first procedure. The conventional open repair was considered to be a high risk for this patient. We therefore performed an alternative hybrid aortic procedure. The supraarch vessels were perfused through an extraanatomic bypass from an unusual and remote route, namely, the abdominal aorta. Then, stent grafts were implanted in the previously replaced aortic prostheses to exclude the aortic aneurysm. The patient recovered uneventfully with no neurologic adverse events."
      },
